Subject: register VM: dedicated threaded handlers for the measured cold opcodes

What this does:
  tools/regvm/threadcover.py reads the per-row ##ROP## histograms and names
  every opcode above 0.5% of dispatches that still dispatches through
  regvm_t_cold. Each one gets its own threaded handler here: load_i32,
  add_imm_s/sub_imm_s, add_imm_self_s/sub_imm_self_s, put_local_xfer_s0..s3,
  object_template, array_from, put_global, get_length, put_element,
  object_fill, get_arg_el, local_rmw_branch, tail_call and tail_call_method.
  The handler shape matches the rest of the threaded set: the common case is
  inline and frameless, and the half that must call the engine is a musttail
  slow path. tail_call/tail_call_method mirror regvm_t_return, ending the
  frame through regvm_t_return_common, and hand a generator frame back to the
  monolith.

Why we need it:
  regvm_t_cold costs two tail calls plus the regvm_exec_one switch per
  dispatch. deltablue spent ~16% of its cycles there (tail_call_method 2.9%,
  get_length 2.1%), richards 2.9%, typescript 2.7%, pdfjs 7.2% (load_i32 2.3%),
  splay 11.8% (object_fill/object_template/sub_imm_s/array_from), earleyboyer
  4.8% (put_global 1.5%). Threading them cuts the unthreaded share to <=1.1%
  on every measured row.

Needs a bytecode version bump:
  No. Runtime only.

Notes:
  Also fixes a real arithmetic bug found while reading the sub_imm_s bodies:
  the int-overflow float fallback added the immediate instead of subtracting it
  (monolith L_ROP_sub_imm_s, L_ROP_sub_imm_self_s and the two cold copies).
  Two differential kernels (sub-imm-ovf, add-imm-ovf) cover it; the corpus is
  now 400 cases.
  Box2d checksum 546.1008391043414, test262 50/81192 threaded+record,
  differential corpus 400/0/0. Octane geomean cycles 0.9645 of the previous
  threaded build (no row worse than +0.7%).

